[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr. stdout and stderr matched (both empty); only the exit status differed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ echo 5 | ./c_src/build/driver | true                 # ${PIPESTATUS[1]} == 141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ echo 5 | ./translation/target/release/driver | true   # ${PIPESTATUS[1]} == 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs, so a failing write returns `EPIPE` instead of terminating the process.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The translation additionally discarded the `write_all`/`flush` results, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m error was invisible. A C program inherits the default disposition, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m terminates it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` now restores the default `SIGPIPE`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disposition (`signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)`, declared via a plain `extern "C"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m block so no new dependency is needed) as the first thing `main` does. This is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a no-op whenever stdout is writable, and reproduces C's signal death when it is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Regression coverage: `stdout_pipe_with_no_reader`. The test closes the read end
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m of the child's stdout *before* writing its stdin — the child blocks in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `read(0)` until then — so it is deterministic rather than racy. Reverting the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fix makes that test fail with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `C ExitStatus(unix_wait_status(13)) vs Rust ExitStatus(unix_wait_status(0))`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m which confirms the test exercises the bug.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors deliberately preserved, not "fixed"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are C/glibc quirks that the translation reproduces on purpose. None was a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mismatch; they are recorded because they look like bugs and a future reader may
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m be tempted to correct them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` failure leaves `x` alone.** The return value of `scanf` is never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   checked, so on EOF or a matching failure the program prints the hex of the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   initial `0` rather than reporting an error. Empty input is therefore a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   *success* path producing `00000000` and exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **glibc converts `%d` with `strtol`, which saturates.** An out-of-range digit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   string does not wrap: it becomes `LONG_MAX` / `LONG_MIN`, which is *then*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   truncated to `int`. So `"99999999999999999999"` yields `-1` (`ffffffff`) and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"-99999999999999999999"` yields `0` (`00000000`) — a wrapping accumulator
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   would give different answers for both. The translation mirrors the saturate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   then truncate order.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Values that fit a 64-bit `long` truncate silently.** `"2147483648"` becomes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `INT_MIN` and `"4294967296"` becomes `0`; `scanf` still reports success.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Host byte order is observable.** `print_hex` dumps the object
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   representation of the `int`, so on x86-64 the output is little-endian
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`42` → `2a000000`). The translation uses `to_ne_bytes`, matching the C on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   whatever host it is built for rather than hard-coding a byte order.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` skips whitespace across newlines**, unlike `fgets`. The leading-
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   whitespace skip is unbounded and consumes `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\v` and `\f`, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   a value on the fifth line is read as readily as one on the first.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Only the first token is consumed.** The rest of stdin, however long or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   malformed, is never examined.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`argv` is ignored** — `main` takes no parameters.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Write errors other than SIGPIPE are swallowed.** C's `exit`-time flush
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   discards its error, so `> /dev/full` exits 0; the translation also ignores
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the `write_all` result. Verified identical.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
